        "page-4-r0001"
      ],
      "text": "Resistivity depends on temperature — usually in metals",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0002",
      "normalized_math": "R\\uparrow,\\quad T\\uparrow",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0002"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Resistance increases as temperature increases.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0003",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0003"
      ],
      "text": "(Superconductors — materials with zero resistance — are generally extremely low temperature. “High temperature” superconductors are those which exhibit",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0004",
      "normalized_math": "R=0",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0004"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Resistance equals zero.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0005",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0005"
      ],
      "text": "above 77 K, the boiling point of nitrogen.)",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0006",
      "level": 1,
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0006"
      ],
      "text": "Ohmic Materials (Every material in 1B)",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0007",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0007"
      ],
      "text": "Ohmic materials obey Ohm’s Law:",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0008",
      "normalized_math": "I=\\frac{V}{R}",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0009"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Current equals voltage divided by resistance.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0009",
      "normalized_math": "I=\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\right)V",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0008"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Current equals one over resistance times voltage.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "complex",
      "detailed_figure_description": "A coordinate graph has current I on the vertical axis and voltage V on the horizontal axis. Two blue straight lines pass through the origin with positive slopes; one is steep and the other is shallow. The graph illustrates that an ohmic material has a linear current-voltage relationship and that increasing resistance decreases the slope.",
      "figure_alternative": "Current-versus-voltage graph for ohmic materials, showing two straight lines through the origin with different slopes.",
      "id": "page-4-s0010",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0010"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0011",
      "normalized_math": "\\text{as }R\\uparrow,\\quad \\frac{\\Delta I}{\\Delta V}\\downarrow",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0011"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "As resistance increases, the change in current divided by the change in voltage decreases.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0012",
      "level": 2,
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0012"
      ],
      "text": "Ohm’s Law:",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0013",
      "normalized_math": "V=IR",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0013"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Voltage equals current times resistance.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0014",
      "level": 2,
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0015"
      ],
      "text": "Example",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0015",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0014"
      ],
      "text": "In the circuit below, is V higher at point A or B? What about I?",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "complex",
      "detailed_figure_description": "A single-loop circuit contains a resistor in the top wire and a battery in the bottom wire. Point A is immediately to the left of the resistor, and point B is immediately to its right. An orange arrow above the resistor indicates current I flowing from A toward B. The battery’s positive terminal is on the left and its negative terminal is on the right.",
      "figure_alternative": "Series circuit with current flowing from point A through a resistor to point B; the battery is positive on the left and negative on the right.",
      "id": "page-4-s0016",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0016"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0017",
      "normalized_math": "V=IR,\\quad \\text{so}\\quad V_A>V_B",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0017"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Voltage equals current times resistance, so the voltage at A is greater than the voltage at B.",
      "type": "formula"
    }
